WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.030
Marvel Studios' She-Hulk
Attorney at Law has some

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.160
smashing visual
effects, which is why

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.560
I'm speaking with VFX
supervisor Shannon

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.080
Justison and Dadi Einarsson.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.310
Hey folks.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.870
All right, let's jump in.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.560
Now there are some unbelievable
visual effects in the series.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.120
But what's the most
interesting thing

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.010
about working on the series?

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.920
I think the most interesting
thing for doing the VFX

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.110
for a show like this is
that we have a CG lead

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.690
and that has never been
done on a Marvel television

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.610
show before because
it's a little crazy.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.280
I mean it's a huge show.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.150
There are digital
humans involved.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.410
So you're creating something
using a lot of input data

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:38.880
and a lot of
performance capture.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.550
But ultimately
it's a CG creation

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.910
that's going to be acting
alongside actual human actors.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.180
It was all about making sure
that we had everything in place

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.850
to get the best
performance recorded,

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.210
so that we could deliver on
a she-hulk character that

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.020
represented Tatiana's
acting ability.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.110
Still in control No the
overwhelming feelings of rage?

00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:58.910
No.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:00.240
A normal amount of rage.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.460
So you know walking into that
room you were going to have

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.580
a fully CG lead character.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.790
Where do you start?

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.120
So if you're going to create
a fully digital character like

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.040
She-Hulk, there's sort of
two streams you're pursuing

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.130
at the same time.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.370
The first is Visdev,
which would be standard

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.470
for any sort of CG character.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.890
We have a visual development
department here at Marvel.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.630
They look at all the comic
books and they look at how

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.430
our story is telling her story.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.550
And then the other side of that
is that we look at our casting.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.200
The important part
of this is that we

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.940
need to know what
Tatiana looks like

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.610
in order to be able to transform
her in and out of She-Hulk.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:36.420
We in VFX subject her to a lot
of scanning and photography.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.880
That then goes to
build stage and that

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.370
goes through multiple
steps of development,

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:45.270
making the model look as
real as possible, just

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.190
in terms of like a
physical proportions

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.970
and the physical aspect of it.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:50.700
So once you've done
all of that digital work

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.020
you have Tatiana
Maslany performance.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.220
How do you pair the CG and
her performance together?

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.410
We captured petabytes of data.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.050
It was absurd how much
data we pulled off the set.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.750
We actually had a really
big on set VFX team

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.060
just because we had so much
data acquisition to do.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.430
When we're recording
mocap and H.M.C., on set,

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.110
we've got several sets
of data that we're

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.310
actually kind of picking out.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.430
So the mocap data
actually records not video

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.690
but it is straight data.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:19.080
And that is purely data that's
recorded to a digital skeleton,

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.690
so if you had walked over
to the mocap brain bar

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.270
while we were recording, you
could see a little skeleton

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.430
running around doing Tatiana's
or Mark's performances,

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:28.860
which is pretty wild.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.110
Once you have all of
that data and footage,

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.020
how do you bring them
together into a scene

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.690
to create a realistic
She-Hulk performance?

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.580
In order to make
She-Hulk more lifelike,

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.290
we always start with
the animation first,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.150
because for a
character like this,

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:44.400
that's the foundation of
everything that is to follow.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.050
I mean, there
are so many things

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.670
that we consider when
we're trying to make

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.770
her feel as real as possible.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:54.600
We try to make it as close to
what Tatiana did as possible,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.240
and really see what
the essence of each one

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.230
of those performances is.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:00.750
So starting from
the edit, then we

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.610
can start working from selects.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.980
Based on the selects,
we would for instance

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.690
we say we have a She-Hulk shot.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.770
This is the shot that
the editors have chosen.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.260
This is the mocap performance.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.450
All that goes over
to a vendor and then

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:15.480
they start their process
of tracking the plates,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.000
ingesting the mocap, which
means transferring the mocap

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.710
data to the She-Hulk rig.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.660
And yeah, the thing
that's most exciting

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.460
is just seeing it come together.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:24.540
Seeing it come to life.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.200
It's a long process.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:29.550
We would start by post vising,
and where Tatiana would

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.590
be walking around
in her mocap suit,

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:35.490
we would then put in a She-Hulk
in kind of a low Res quick turn

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.850
around post this form so that
could start to inform our edit.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.010
While the animation
team is working away,

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:43.170
the shaders team is
working on refining

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.020
all of the look of her
skin texture, her hair

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.610
texture, her clothing texture.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.020
We're building out her clothes.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.430
We're building out her shoes.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.890
Then really once you
get turnover and start

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:56.580
to build that scene and see
the things coming to life,

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.110
you start to match the lighting.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.720
You start to see the performance
go on the actual She-Hulk

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:01.890
animation puppet.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:05.200
It's super exciting seeing that
character come to life once.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.220
Once have an animated
performance that we like

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.410
and the studio buys
off on, at that point

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.770
we start all of what we call
the Sims, the simulations.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:17.010
That's where we start the
clothing simulations, the hair

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.839
simulations, and
because all of that

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:20.550
is driven by the
animation, we don't

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.820
really start that until after
the animation is complete.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.170
She-Hulk has so
many different looks.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:27.540
I imagine that's a lot of work.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.490
How typical is it
for a CG character

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.380
to have that many outfits?

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.840
Yeah so She-Hulk
has 25 outfits,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.720
which is crazy amount
of different outfits,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.090
like usually CG characters
will have one, maybe two.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:43.560
She has to be in court.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:44.910
She has to be casual.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.830
She has to be kind
of working out.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.330
But you still get
to have a lot of fun

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.770
with like how do the
clothes transform?

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:51.043
What happens to her shoes?

00:04:51.043 --> 00:04:52.410
DADI EINARSSON: Each
one of these outfits

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.660
has to be digitally created.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.520
It has to have its simulation.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.110
There'll be multiple
layers to it.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.890
In one of our episodes,
She-Hulk turns up

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:04.040
at a Gala event for
female lawyer of the year,

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.220
and she is wearing
a sequenced dress.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:09.350
She-Hulk's Gala
dress the sequin dress.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:14.580
It's incredibly complicated
and terrifying to build in CG.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.830
DADI EINARSSON: The
dress is mind blowing.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.390
It is so amazingly well done.

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:21.620
I think there are over a
million sequins that sparkle.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:22.940
They catch the light correctly.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.800
They have to be
form fitting on her.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:27.290
They have to flow with the
dress and I'm just constantly

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.300
aware of when I'm
reviewing those shots

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.460
just how like what
an achievement it

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:32.700
was to make that dress.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:33.500
Oh my God.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.300
That thing is incredible.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:36.710
It's so well done.

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:39.830
It really is so incredible,
and the whole series

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:41.960
really is so special.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:43.580
What are you most
proud of looking

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.290
back at this entire series?

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:49.010
I'm really excited for
people to see this character.

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:52.340
I'm excited for kids,
my kids to see her.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.820
And yeah, just being involved
with of bringing to the screen

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:59.150
so many shots of
a digital human.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.150
I am very grateful for
the opportunity to be

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:03.920
involved with something so big.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:05.240
I really feel like
it's been an honor

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.450
to be part of the she-hulk team,
especially as the VFX team.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:12.200
I love origin stories and
she's such a fun and like witty

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:13.340
and she's meta.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.510
And she's just such
a great character.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:19.410
So to be a part of her origin
story is just a privilege.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.800
Oh, Shannon and Dadi, it was
such a blast getting to talk

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:23.750
with you a bit
more about She-Hulk

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.140
and how you bring her to life.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.180
Thank you so much.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.240
And of course everyone make
sure you go watch all episodes

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.700
of Marvel Studios'
She-Hulk: Attorney at Law

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:35.050
now streaming only
on Disney Plus.

